=0$\n- **III.** $f(t)⊥g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133867 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e37d21-2b65-5624-8c6f-3a3fcb9d5df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327560} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe3b2c-937b-5969-b4b2-43d693fa584f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328470} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6004e5-518c-5445-889a-b05d85f3ae0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328707} {:answer/id #uuid "ec737965-0e20-58db-998d-b1f04996bb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329005} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4ff121-c45f-51ef-b9c0-e2a6bfbf7e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec737965-0e20-58db-998d-b1f04996bb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329005} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a4a86f-1208-5201-973f-39806b593711" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e996ecb-6acb-5d32-96b4-9b95b6fb1647" :question/math-academy-id "q-44069" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1]\\,,$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=k-2{t}^{3}$ and $y(t)=t$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product is equal to zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(k-2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(kt-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{k{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}-\\frac{2}{5} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ k & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a9f1c8-05ef-5001-a4c6-0d7ab1450caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358193} {:answer/id #uuid "5f34318b-0098-52c8-b1de-d0299d8575b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358581} {:answer/id #uuid "64a691d6-e141-5bb7-9ee6-4264932cc4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358632} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3b6e75-e271-5097-ae36-92029f685b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358843} {:answer/id #uuid "857d12af-9218-5469-b698-e7a8a45e2585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3b6e75-e271-5097-ae36-92029f685b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358843} :answer-field/id #uuid "67bd4b2c-222f-5e1b-bc32-eeed6de84d4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c0fc30-88bd-5a7f-af92-f6bc1add957b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97348" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+4{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot k+3\\cdot ((-3)\\cdot (-5))+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5k+45+5 \\\\  & =5k+50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5k+50 & =0 \\\\ 5k & =-50 \\\\ k & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133868 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46ebecec-2507-5893-8529-69e7bbd61765" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f101e5-cfde-5208-a26b-ab71b5c23c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357804} {:answer/id #uuid "5754b3e1-3795-5306-80bf-207c94c5a09e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358511} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc14cd5-3823-5921-bc6b-e06f6933e12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358986} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d76379-04f8-532e-82e1-c00042f2ce4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359310} {:answer/id #uuid "b61eb43e-f953-5220-9d20-715d7ae86825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05f101e5-cfde-5208-a26b-ab71b5c23c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357804} :answer-field/id #uuid "fba4033f-ed46-5b60-85b2-f4dcbe684d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09585884-a23f-5d81-a9d8-5d380fd1cbe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97353" :question/problem "Let $V={R}^{3}$ be the vector space equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ and $y$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+5\\cdot ((-5)\\cdot 3)+2\\cdot (k\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =-15-75+10k \\\\  & =-90+10k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x,y⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =0 \\\\ -90+10k & =0 \\\\ 10k & =90 \\\\ k & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133866 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12723510-d0ac-5272-83ed-d88ef0709923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357910} {:answer/id #uuid "3e81a766-2d25-5e92-bc85-b2a70d25d7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358287} {:answer/id #uuid "63f41823-f64d-56f3-9b0f-3053deb1bb9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 175921863