 in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b61f82-a48e-5523-85c3-1b111693c420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357933} {:answer/id #uuid "638f7e3d-c1bb-5d8a-ad03-48fb8a2d5c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358619} {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ea961-2c23-51f2-a9b4-7babef739b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359003} {:answer/id #uuid "be66fb5a-bc19-5d6a-89f8-cbc7bb59e982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359415} {:answer/id #uuid "ea993918-14ee-5ab7-b005-1cdbe929bf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8ea961-2c23-51f2-a9b4-7babef739b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359003} :answer-field/id #uuid "963385f8-34a8-55e4-807e-c57faeed9c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be1a192e-2faf-5418-a76f-14cd5ed63a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-97360" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+\\frac{1}{10}x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=3+t$ and $q(t)=3t-k{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(0)q(0)+2p(1)q(1)+\\frac{1}{10}p(2)q(2) \\\\  & =(3+0)(0)+(3+1)(3-k)+\\frac{1}{10}(3+2)(6-4k) \\\\  & =0+(12-4k)+(3-2k) \\\\  & =15-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 15-6k & =0 \\\\ 6k & =15 \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133866 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a348cfec-2c8f-5302-a94a-43a6c9330c34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54e3f171-dd1e-55f6-b1e3-aa21977c2b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358486} {:answer/id #uuid "79bb7b13-3aac-5627-bfd3-79156842a3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358811} {:answer/id #uuid "88fb6f8c-9833-5458-a58e-daf64a217c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358948} {:answer/id #uuid "b43f34b9-745a-5ef2-a11f-697509c05ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359321} {:answer/id #uuid "b4af7d43-9537-5e75-a075-83a66ead02af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4af7d43-9537-5e75-a075-83a66ead02af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359324} :answer-field/id #uuid "921d66e