ents of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f938404-6535-5333-be8e-56e51efd4e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357878} {:answer/id #uuid "3881ede7-2355-50c2-8deb-b3e57afa9b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358253} {:answer/id #uuid "771279f7-d7d8-5627-80d7-cb860874564a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358777} {:answer/id #uuid "a23fccf5-c8da-50b9-bfd1-5436127bdfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359168} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bf4633-c0c9-515d-a8e0-7121d132b5fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f938404-6535-5333-be8e-56e51efd4e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357878} :answer-field/id #uuid "60bf0cea-0196-5ac5-922c-b876afd8aeda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10a41eac-4d39-5c59-85ee-b6fa71b11cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97363" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2,$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(4)y(4)+x(-4)y(-4)+x(-5)y(-5).\n$$\n\nIf $p(t)=k+t$ and $q(t)=1+t-{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p(t)$ and $q(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =p(4)q(4)+p(-4)q(-4)+p(-5)q(-5) \\\\  & =(k+4)(1+4-16)+(k-4)(1-4-16)+(k-5)(1-5-25) \\\\  & =(-11k-44)+(-19k+76)+(-29k+145) \\\\  & =-59k+177\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨p(t),q(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ -59k+177 & =0 \\\\ 59k & =177 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133867 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41ef25dc-e218-53d7-a1d7-39b5de446835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358310} {:answer/id #uuid "58e969b0-b73d-5e52-95c0-2f0deb6e7beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358524} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb4b9d8-3bd9-59cc-9fba-018773f6813f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358995} {:answer/id #uuid "90d9cc42-62d3-5a6a-b293-ecbb57193d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359021} {:answer/id #uuid "f0af856b-4db0-5119-a778-adaa30b0718c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41ef25dc-e218-53d7-a1d7-39b5de446835" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358310} :answer-field/id #uuid "58844677-7f64-5926-b9e9-f823b9019512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81d22276-2b6e-5fad-b0ea-dfad72ebe3fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-97440" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIf $x(t)=5{t}^{2}-2$ and $y(t)=k+3{t}^{2}$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ in $V,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are orthogonal, their inner product must be zero.\n\nFirst, we compute the inner product using our definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5{t}^{2}-2)(k+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{4}+(5k-6){t}^{2}-2k)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{t}^{5}+\\frac{(5k-6){t}^{3}}{3}-2kt){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=0$ and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{(5k-6)}{3}-2k & =0 \\\\ 9+(5k-6)-6k & =0 \\\\ 3-k & =0 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133867 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "404853dd-2cdf-5ee0-b081-ebd6b8a90ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of Orthogonal Vectors in Inner Product Spaces of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccd4eedd-785c-50cc-b42b-d0c55d4dc7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133375} {:answer/id #uuid "09ad71cc-daf3-58b7-84a2-09b46abc76fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133376} {:answer/id 